WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.603
DNS maps names to services and publishes supporting information.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:07.496
An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address,

00:00:07.496 --> 00:00:10.610
while an AAAA record maps it to IPv6.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.724
A CNAME record aliases one name to another,

00:00:13.724 --> 00:00:16.838
often revealing a hosted platform or service dependency.

00:00:16.838 --> 00:00:18.785
MX records identify mail exchangers.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:21.120
NS records identify authoritative name servers.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.181
TXT records carry text used for purposes such as domain verification, email-security policy,

00:00:26.181 --> 00:00:27.348
and service configuration.

00:00:27.348 --> 00:00:32.409
A TXT value should be interpreted according to its specific standard and context.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.912
Together, records can describe public service names, mail providers,

00:00:35.912 --> 00:00:37.859
verification relationships, and third-party dependencies.

00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:42.919
They can also expose stale configurations or inconsistent ownership that deserves internal review.

00:00:42.919 --> 00:00:44.087
Interpret DNS cautiously.

00:00:44.087 --> 00:00:49.147
Records may be cached, changed, split by resolver location, fronted by a proxy,

00:00:49.147 --> 00:00:51.094
or delegated to a provider.

00:00:51.094 --> 00:00:56.154
One destination can serve many customers, and one service can use many addresses.

00:00:56.154 --> 00:00:58.490
A record is an observed configuration,

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.993
not automatic proof of who operates the underlying system.

00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:02.772
Time matters.

00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:07.832
Note when the record was observed and whether the source represents current, historical,

00:01:07.832 --> 00:01:09.389
passive, or authoritative data.

00:01:09.389 --> 00:01:12.892
Independent sources with different collection paths can increase confidence,

00:01:12.892 --> 00:01:16.785
but repeated copies of one record are not independent corroboration.

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:21.456
Defensive analysis should reconcile public DNS with approved asset inventories, service owners,

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:22.624
and vendor records.

00:01:22.624 --> 00:01:26.517
The useful outcome is a documented question or remediation item,

00:01:26.517 --> 00:01:30.020
not an unsupported conclusion based on a single lookup.
